---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { AUTHOR_NAME } from '../consts';
---

<Layout
	title="About"
	description="Who writes WageLark, where the wage data comes from, and what this site does and doesn't try to do."
>
	<div class="mx-auto max-w-[720px]">
		<h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-[var(--navy)] md:text-4xl">About WageLark</h1>

		<div class="prose-content mt-7 max-w-[640px] text-[0.9375rem] leading-[1.85] text-[var(--ink)]/70">
			<p>
				WageLark answers one question, occupation by occupation: what does this job actually pay,
				what does it take to get into it, and what does the work involve. No rankings, no "top 10
				highest-paying jobs" clickbait. Just the government's own wage and employment numbers,
				laid out clearly and cited back to the source.
			</p>

			<h2>Where the numbers come from</h2>
			<p>
				Every wage figure on this site comes from the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics (BLS),
				specifically the Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics (OEWS) program and the
				Occupational Outlook Handbook (OOH), both public domain federal government data. BLS
				surveys employers directly rather than relying on self-reported salary data, which is why
				we use it as the primary source instead of crowdsourced salary sites.
			</p>
			<p>
				Every page states the exact data year (BLS typically publishes new annual figures each
				May) and links directly to the source page, so you can verify any number yourself. When we
				cite a secondary source (for licensing or certification requirements, for example), it's
				labeled separately from BLS wage data.
			</p>

			<h2>What this site doesn't do</h2>
			<p>
				WageLark reports data. It does not tell you which career to choose, whether a specific
				training program or bootcamp is worth the cost, or whether a career change makes sense for
				your situation. Those are personal decisions that depend on circumstances a wage table
				can't capture. Where we describe education or certification paths, we're describing the
				generally required path per BLS and the relevant licensing/certification bodies, not
				recommending a specific school or program.
			</p>

			<p class="mb-4 text-[var(--ink)]">
				This site is published by the {AUTHOR_NAME}.
			</p>
			<p class="m-0 text-[var(--ink)]">
				Questions, corrections, or press inquiries:
				<a href="mailto:contact@wagelark.com" class="text-[var(--teal-dark)] underline">contact@wagelark.com</a>
			</p>
		</div>
	</div>
</Layout>

<style>
	.prose-content :global(h2) {
		margin-top: 1.75rem;
		margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
		font-size: 1.1rem;
		font-weight: 700;
		color: var(--navy);
	}
	.prose-content :global(h2:first-child) {
		margin-top: 0;
	}
	.prose-content :global(p) {
		margin-bottom: 1rem;
	}
	.prose-content :global(a) {
		color: var(--teal-dark);
		text-decoration: underline;
	}
</style>
